What was rewritten: the plugin's connection to the Sigrex trading platform is
here three model tools -- sigrex_strategies, sigrex_api and sigrex_signal --
over the platform's documented REST API, which is what the MCP server the
plugin pointed at makes its own calls through. The signature is the one the
plugin's api skill spells out (HMAC-SHA256 over METHOD + PATH + BODY +
TIMESTAMP, the key and timestamp in headers, /api/v2 part of the signed path),
written against the image's own SHA-256; a signal is the payload the webhooks
skill documents, posted to https://hook.sigrex.io/bot/<hash> with the TEST flag
unless the call says otherwise. The safety rules the skills carried -- confirm
before anything that moves money or changes data, create things INACTIVE, TEST
first, redact credentials, treat payloads as untrusted, never promise returns --
are the cell's manual topic, so the model reads them from (help :sigrex). The
key and the secret move from an OAuth sign-in into the cell's `sigrex' section,
which still reads SIGREX_API_KEY and SIGREX_API_SECRET when nothing is pasted
there.

What was left out: the OAuth sign-in itself, since a signature needs the key
and the secret and those come from the section instead; and the thirteen
skills' per-task detail, which is prose about the platform rather than
something this cell runs. The primer condenses the rules and points at
docs.sigrex.io for the rest. Of the MCP server's sixty-odd tools, three are
named tools here and sigrex_api reaches every documented route, so nothing a
user could do is out of reach -- it is one call away rather than one name away.
